<%# ** ONE ROW: AN ACCOUNT, WHAT IS IN IT, AND SAVE (account-openings spec §3). ** The same form in
    both places it is asked for — a row in the "Your accounts" card for an account that has not said
    yet, and the **Edit balance** row on a finished account's own card — because they are the same
    act a day apart: `AccountOpening` writes the record the first time and rewrites it every time
    after, so a second spelling of this form would be a second door to one object.

    THE FIELD IS THE ONLY QUESTION THE APP ASKS ABOUT AN ACCOUNT. Not "how much do you want to move
    here", not "what should main be corrected to" — both of those were onboarding steps of their own
    and both are deleted. Henry, 2026-09-06: "they just want to put in what their accounts are
    currently worth."

    Locals: `account` (the Pool this row is about), `url` and `method` (create for a first answer,
    update for a correction — `AccountOpeningsController` treats them identically), `value` (what the
    field opens on: today's balance on a correction, the refused figure after a 422, blank on a first
    answer), `name` (the account's name, shown in the card where the row is the only thing naming it
    and nil under a card header that already does), `data` (the caller's own hook).

    `simple_form_for :account_opening` ON A SYMBOL, not on the AccountOpening object: the refusals
    this door can produce are about the FIGURE — unparsable, or negative off main — and both are
    stated once, in the flash, over a page that re-renders every row. An object-backed form would
    print the same sentence twice on the one row and nothing on the others. %>
<%= simple_form_for :account_opening, url: url, method: method, html: { data: data } do |f| %>
  <%# STACKED BELOW `sm`, one line from `sm` up — the same shape every other inline form on this
      screen uses. `sm:pb-2` sits the name on the input's own baseline rather than at the top of a
      hint that may wrap; `sm:pt-7` drops the button to that line by the label's height. %>
  <div class="px-6 py-4 flex flex-col sm:flex-row sm:items-start gap-3">
    <% if name.present? %>
      <div class="sm:w-1/3 sm:pt-7 text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900 truncate" title="<%= name %>">
        <%= name %>
      </div>
    <% end %>
    <div class="flex-1">
      <%# ** THE FIELD'S id CARRIES THE ACCOUNT'S (measured in the browser, three rows on one card).
          ** `simple_form_for :account_opening` names every input `account_opening_balance`, and this
          partial renders once per unanswered account — so the card shipped three inputs with ONE id
          between them, every `<label for>` pointing at the first. Clicking the third row's label put
          the cursor in the first row's box. The label follows the input rather than the attribute. %>
      <% field_id = "account_opening_balance_#{account.id}" %>
      <%# `min` ON EVERY ACCOUNT BUT MAIN, which is `AccountOpening`'s own rule drawn on the control:
          a mirror cannot be overdrawn by construction, while an overdrawn checking account is a fact
          (spec §4). The server refuses it either way — `config.browser_validations` is off app-wide
          and a bare POST bypasses the browser entirely — so this is a hint, not the guard. %>
      <%= f.input :balance,
          as: :decimal,
          label: "What's in it right now",
          placeholder: "0.00",
          required: false,
          label_html: { for: field_id },
          input_html: { id: field_id, step: 0.01, value: value, **(account.main? ? {} : { min: 0 }) },
          hint: "What your bank shows for this account today." %>
    </div>
    <div class="sm:pt-7">
      <%= f.button :submit, "Save", class: "btn btn-primary" %>
    </div>
  </div>
<% end %>
